WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.853
A critical thinking process often starts with a simple question: what is the claim?

00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:07.229
A claim is what someone is asserting.

00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:11.303
It may be a factual statement, an interpretation, a prediction, a recommendation,

00:00:11.303 --> 00:00:13.000
or a judgment about value.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.055
Before evaluating evidence, make sure the claim is visible.

00:00:16.055 --> 00:00:21.487
Many arguments become tangled because people are actually discussing different claims while using the same words.

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:24.542
Facts, interpretations, opinions, predictions, and recommendations need different treatment.

00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:27.258
A fact claim asks whether something is true.

00:00:27.258 --> 00:00:29.634
An interpretation explains what the facts mean.

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:32.011
An opinion expresses a preference or judgment.

00:00:32.011 --> 00:00:34.047
A prediction estimates what will happen.

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:36.424
A recommendation says what should be done.

00:00:36.424 --> 00:00:40.497
Each can be useful, but each requires a different kind of support.

00:00:40.497 --> 00:00:42.195
Once the claim is clear,

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:46.268
ask what would need to be true for the claim to hold.

00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:48.984
This question exposes the logic of the argument.

00:00:48.984 --> 00:00:52.718
If someone says a new tool will reduce incident response time,

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:54.416
what assumptions are built in?

00:00:54.416 --> 00:00:57.132
Does the team have capacity to deploy it?

00:00:57.132 --> 00:00:58.829
Will alerts improve or multiply?

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:01.884
Does the claim depend on training, integration, process change,

00:01:01.884 --> 00:01:04.600
or vendor data that has not been verified?

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.618
Evidence quality matters.

00:01:05.618 --> 00:01:07.995
Relevant evidence connects directly to the claim.

00:01:07.995 --> 00:01:11.729
Sufficient evidence is enough to support the strength of the conclusion.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:13.426
Missing evidence should reduce confidence.

00:01:13.426 --> 00:01:15.463
Repetition is not evidence by itself.

00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:17.500
Confidence is not evidence by itself.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.895
A dashboard, case study, anecdote, or expert statement may help,

00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:23.950
but each should be judged by source, method, context,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.987
and fit to the actual decision.

00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:27.684
Burden of proof also matters.

00:01:27.684 --> 00:01:32.097
The stronger or more consequential the claim, the more support it should require.

00:01:32.097 --> 00:01:36.511
A low-risk hypothesis may only need enough evidence to justify a small experiment.

00:01:36.511 --> 00:01:40.245
A high-stakes operational change deserves stronger evidence, clearer assumptions, and review.

00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:43.300
Strong conclusions require more than volume, fluency, or certainty.
